+# Decision: Change delivery route
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS and the server assigns each committed edit one event revision. How a committed revision travels from the server to the up-to-800 staff browsers watching an event is a separate question, and it is the one this decision settles.
+
+The route is load-bearing well beyond its own component. Three design nodes are shaped by it: the change store (what it must retain and how it hands changes onward), delivery (what it owns at runtime), and browser sync (how a browser subscribes and recovers). Choosing wrong is expensive not because the board would be unusable — every option below can show changes inside the 15-second goal — but because the route dictates the internal structure of three components and the operational surface the project carries during event hours. Reversing it later means rewriting those three nodes, not swapping a library.
+
+Several project constraints bear on the choice without separating the options, and are background rather than decision elements. Every option can meet the 15-second freshness goal, support 800 simultaneous browsers, and preserve one authoritative revision per committed edit. Event updates carry stall IDs and operational statuses, not customer records, so no data-sensitivity rule disqualifies a third-party provider. No option was eliminated outright; all three were genuinely in contention, and the decision turns on weighing the criteria below.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gates apply: the constraints that could have eliminated an option are all satisfied by all three (see Context).
+
+**Update latency** — *moderate.* Time from commit to a connected browser reflecting the change. The goal fixes 15 seconds as sufficient under normal connectivity. Subsecond updates are useful — a coordinator watching a board during an opening-time burst benefits from immediacy — but nothing in the goals requires them, which caps this criterion's weight.
+
+**Vendor independence** — *major.* How much of the delivery path the project can change without a provider's cooperation, and what it would cost to leave. The board is operational infrastructure during published event hours across forty markets; a provider's pricing change, availability incident, or subscription-model revision would reach into delivery and browser-sync intent, not just a billing line.
+
+**Recurring operating cost** — *moderate.* Ongoing spend at pilot scale: 800 simultaneous browsers, forty markets, two status changes per minute with bursts to 20 edits per second. Material but not decisive — no cost ceiling has been declared for the pilot.
+
+**Operational burden carried in-house** — *moderate.* Work the project must staff to keep the route healthy: connection capacity planning, reconnect behavior under mass disconnection, and the failure modes that come with holding state per connected browser. This criterion cuts against the chosen option.
+
+**Failure visibility** — *major.* How directly the route distinguishes "the board has not changed" from "this browser is no longer receiving changes." Turns on the tenet *connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board* — a coordinator acting on a silently stale board is the failure the tenet exists to prevent.
+
+**Catch-up cost after disconnection** — *minor.* What the route requires in order to recover the latest board after up to 20 minutes offline. Every option needs replayable revisions to meet that goal; the options differ only in how much of the catch-up path is already built.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers poll an HTTPS endpoint every five seconds, passing the last revision they hold; the server answers with the revisions committed since that cursor, or an empty response. The server keeps no per-browser state — each poll is an ordinary request/response against the change store's revision history. The change store needs cursor queries over revisions; delivery is a request handler with nothing to manage between calls; browsers run a timer loop and advance their cursor. This is the whole mechanism: there is no connection to lose, no session registry, and nothing to reconcile after a restart.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency: **partial** fit. Adds up to five seconds of polling lag on top of commit time, well inside the 15-second goal but visibly behind a pushed update during bursts.
+- Vendor independence: **strong** fit. No third party in the path; the mechanism is plain HTTPS against the project's own store.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong** fit. No realtime service fees. 800 browsers polling at five-second intervals is a steady 160 requests per second of mostly-empty responses against the change store.
+- Operational burden carried in-house: **strong** fit. A stateless endpoint scales behind an ordinary load balancer and has no connection lifecycle to operate.
+- Failure visibility: **partial** fit. A failed or timed-out poll is directly observable by the browser, so staleness is detectable; an empty response and a lost server are distinguishable only by whether the request itself succeeded, which is sufficient but leaves the browser to track consecutive failures.
+- Catch-up cost after disconnection: **strong** fit. Recovery is the normal path — a browser that was away for 20 minutes polls with an old cursor and receives the same revision range any other poll would.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a few seconds of lag and continuous repeated reads for the simplest possible operation.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** Browsers hold a persistent WebSocket connection to a server the project runs, subscribed to one event. When a command commits, the change store publishes the new revision and the server fans it out to the sessions subscribed to that event. The change store must keep revisions replayable from any revision a browser names and publish committed changes onward reliably, so that a change cannot commit and then fail to reach subscribers unnoticed. Delivery owns the session registry, connection acceptance, fan-out, and a capacity limit with defined behavior when it is reached. Browsers detect a dropped connection, reconnect, and request the revisions committed while they were away. Updates arrive as fast as the server can write them; no third party is involved.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency: **strong** fit. A committed revision is pushed as soon as it commits, with no polling interval in the path.
+- Vendor independence: **strong** fit. The entire delivery path is project-owned and changeable without anyone's cooperation.
+- Recurring operating cost: **strong** fit. No service fees; cost is the compute needed to hold 800 concurrent connections and the hosting it runs on.
+- Operational burden carried in-house: **weak** fit. The project owns connection capacity for 800 simultaneous browsers, the behavior when that capacity is reached, and reconnect correctness — including the reconnect storm that follows any server restart or network event, when a large fraction of browsers reconnect and request catch-up at once.
+- Failure visibility: **strong** fit. Connection state is explicit: a closed socket is an unambiguous signal that this browser is no longer receiving changes, distinct from a quiet board.
+- Catch-up cost after disconnection: **partial** fit. Catch-up is a second path that must be built and kept consistent with the live fan-out path, rather than being the same path the browser already uses.
+
+**Summary.** Trades owned connection and reconnect operations for fast updates with nothing between the server and the browser.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** A third-party realtime provider holds the browser connections. The server publishes each committed revision to the provider, which fans it out to subscribed browsers over its own transport. The change store must reconcile its revision history against what was successfully published, since the authoritative record is local while the fan-out is remote and the two can diverge. Delivery becomes a wrapper around the provider's publish API plus credential issuance for browser subscriptions. Browsers use the provider's client library and its subscription, presence, and reconnect semantics rather than ones the project writes. Connection capacity, scaling, and reconnect handling are the provider's problem.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency: **strong** fit. Pushed delivery, comparable to self-hosted sockets.
+- Vendor independence: **weak** fit. The provider's subscription model reaches into browser-sync intent and its credential model into delivery; leaving means rewriting both nodes against a different transport.
+- Recurring operating cost: **weak** fit. Per-connection or per-message fees across forty markets and 800 simultaneous browsers, recurring for the life of the system.
+- Operational burden carried in-house: **strong** fit. Connection capacity and reconnect behavior are operated by the provider, which is the largest share of the work this route would otherwise require.
+- Failure visibility: **partial** fit. The provider surfaces connection state to the browser, so staleness is visible; the project does not control what that signal means or when it fires, and a publish that fails on the provider's side is visible only through reconciliation.
+- Catch-up cost after disconnection: **partial** fit. The provider supplies some replay, but the 20-minute recovery goal must be met against the project's own revision history regardless, so the reconciliation path exists in addition to whatever the provider offers.
+
+**Summary.** Trades recurring fees and provider coupling for the shortest path to pushed updates with the least connection operations.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.**
+
+It wins on the two major criteria. On **vendor independence** it is tied with polling and clearly ahead of the hosted service: nothing in the delivery path requires a third party's cooperation to change, which matters for a system that forty markets depend on during published event hours. On **failure visibility** it is the strongest of the three — an explicit connection state gives the browser an unambiguous signal that it has stopped receiving changes, which is the most direct expression of the tenet *connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board*; polling infers the same thing from accumulated request failures, and the hosted service reports it through semantics the project does not control.
+
+Against polling, the deciding margin is **update latency**, where polling is only a partial fit. Both routes satisfy the 15-second goal, so this is a preference for immediacy during opening-time bursts rather than a requirement, and it is weighed knowing that polling is the better answer on operational burden.
+
+The cost accepted is exactly that: **operational burden carried in-house** is a weak fit, and this option is chosen over the one that scores strongest there. The project owns connection capacity for 800 simultaneous browsers, the behavior when capacity is reached, and reconnect correctness including the reconnect storm after a restart. That burden was weighed and accepted in exchange for pushed updates with no vendor in the path. The hosted service is the option that would have relieved it, and it is rejected because relief costs recurring fees and couples two design nodes to a provider — a trade against the major criterion.
+
+Committing to this forecloses the stateless-server simplicity polling would have given: the delivery component now holds per-browser state, which makes restarts and capacity a standing operational concern rather than a non-issue. The single-region pilot non-goal keeps that concern bounded, since there is no cross-region session coordination to solve.
+
+Downstream, this obligates three nodes:
+
+- **Change store** — revisions replayable from any revision a browser names, and reliable publication of committed changes onward, so a commit cannot succeed while its fan-out is silently lost.
+- **Delivery** — socket session registry, connection acceptance, per-event subscription, fan-out of committed revisions, and an explicit capacity limit with defined behavior at the limit.
+- **Browser sync** — subscription over the socket, disconnect detection, reconnect, and catch-up from the browser's last known revision, meeting the 20-minute recovery goal.
